#
# RESOLVED (July 2026, the run-collection drop) — kept as a
# regression note.
#
# The problem was: quoted strings crossed two different worlds.
# The lexical table said "..." is guest-boundary text OR a host
# path — and that "or" was the mush: the same spelling was
# host-side in one verb and guest-side in the next, with the
# direction of travel invisible:
#
#   stage "payloads/AUTOTEST.EXE"     # HOST path, forward slashes
#   enter "C:\PAYLOADS\AUTOTEST.EXE"  # GUEST text, backslashes
#   collect "RESULTS.LOG" to="results/"   # both worlds in one line
#
# THE FIX: the file-exchange verbs (`stage`, `collect`, and the
# `results` header) left the language with the run-collection
# model — file exchange is out-of-band host work against a stopped
# machine (script spec, "File exchange — a named omission"). No
# verb takes a host path any more, so every quoted string lives on
# one side of one boundary:

description "One quoting, one world — resolved"
platform    dos
machine     stopped
timeout     2m

start
wait  "A:\>"                    # GUEST text — matched on the screen
enter "AUTOTEST.EXE"            # GUEST text — typed at the prompt
wait  machine=stopped

# The sigil story is now total: @ marks a library reference,
# $ marks a property, and a bare "..." is always guest text.
#
# RESIDUE (none): the open question — whether host paths deserved
# their own spelling — dissolved rather than being answered; if
# file verbs ever re-enter the language, it reopens with them.
